        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {},
          "text": "this is illegal in 90% of cars.\n\nA developer just open sourced an app that turns any Android phone into a CarPlay device, the system Apple charges automakers millions to license.\n\nIt's called OkCar. It tricks your car's head unit into thinking your Android is an iPhone running CarPlay, and your entire dashboard lights up like you just bought into the Apple ecosystem.\n\nEvery Android Auto user knows the pain. You buy a car, the head unit only supports CarPlay, and suddenly your $1,200 Pixel is a worse driving experience than a 2018 iPhone SE. Automakers locked you out and Google never built a workaround.\n\nThis repo is the workaround.\n\nHere's what makes it different from every half-broken CarPlay hack floating around GitHub:\n\n→ Full CarPlay protocol implementation in pure Kotlin, no jailbreak, no rooted phone, no fake hardware dongle\n→ Wireless and wired both supported, plug in over USB or pair over Bluetooth and WiFi\n→ Native Android apps render directly onto the car screen, not just mirrored from your phone\n→ Google Maps, Spotify, WhatsApp, Waze all work on the head unit while your phone stays in your pocket\n→ Voice control routes through Google Assistant instead of Siri, finally\n\nKilled: $200 Carlinkit dongles, $150 Ottocast adapters, every aftermarket CarPlay box charging hardware prices for software emulation. Killed the $30K used car premium people pay just to get a head unit that supports their phone.\n\nWorks on any car built in the last decade with a CarPlay-capable head unit. Toyota, Honda, Ford, BMW, Mercedes, Hyundai, Kia, Mazda, the entire industry.\n\nApache 2.0. 100% Opensource."
        },
